TASK:
Design the power logic for the speaker amplifiers of a Lenovo Yoga Book 9 13IRU8 (82YQ) laptop on Linux, so the amps (and ideally the audio DSP) draw nothing while no sound plays and come up calibrated when sound starts. READ-ONLY investigation: do not change any ALSA control, PipeWire/WirePlumber setting, service or file, do not play audio, no sudo.

Facts on this machine:
- Kernel 7.2.5 (Arch-based Omarchy). Audio card `sof-hda-dsp` (Intel SOF, Raptor Lake, machine driver skl_hda_dsp_generic), Realtek ALC287 HDA codec, two TI TAS2781 smart amps on I2C (`i2c-TIAS2781:00`), modules snd_hda_scodec_tas2781_i2c, snd_soc_tas2781_fmwlib, snd_soc_tas2781_comlib. ALSA kcontrols: numid=1 'Speaker Profile Id', numid=2 'Speaker Analog Volume', numid=3 'Speaker Force Firmware Load', numid=4 'Speaker Program Id', numid=5 'Speaker Config Id', numid=7 'Speaker Playback Switch' (pin 0x17), numid=9 'Bass Speaker Playback Switch' (pin 0x14).
- PipeWire + WirePlumber 0.5. A filter-chain EQ sink `yoga_dolby` feeds `alsa_output.pci-0000_00_1f.3-platform-skl_hda_dsp_generic.HiFi__Speaker__sink`.
- The repo /home/gon7187/Projects/Omarchy-LenovoYogaBook9 (read README.md around lines 285-300 and 975-1000, config/wireplumber/52-yoga-speakers-keep-open.conf, config/udev/90-yoga-tas2781-no-runtime-pm.rules, bin/yoga-amp-calib, bin/yoga-volume) documents: the factory calibration in the UEFI variable CALI_DATA had a zero CRC and was repaired; but calibration is lost again on the first PCM close/open, because tas2781_hda_playback_hook runs the amp shutdown block on CLOSE and power-up on OPEN, while tasdevice_select_tuningprm_cfg rewrites calibration only when the DSP configuration number changes ("Unneeded loading dsp conf"). Workaround in place: session.suspend-timeout-seconds = 0 on the speaker sink, so the PCM never closes after the first sound; plus a udev rule forcing power/control=on for the amps. Toggling 'Speaker Config Id' (1 then 0) is documented to force a reload and re-apply calibration without reboot.
- Measured on battery: speaker PCM closed 8.93 W; PCM held open (silence) 9.55-9.67 W, i.e. ~0.65 W extra, held from the first sound until the next system suspend.

Questions to answer with primary-source evidence (Linux kernel source for 7.2 or newest mainline at git.kernel.org / github.com/torvalds/linux; TI TAS2781 datasheet; WirePlumber 0.5 source/docs), citing files, functions and commits:
1. Where do the 0.65 W go: the TAS2781 amps in active mode playing silence (estimate from the datasheet at a ~8-12 V supply), the SOF DSP/HDA link keeping the SoC out of package C10, PipeWire graph wakeups, or a mix? Which read-only measurement on this machine would split them?
2. Is the calibration-loss bug still present in the 7.2 driver code, or fixed upstream (which commit)? What exactly is the minimal userspace action that guarantees calibrated sound after a PCM reopen, and must it happen before or after OPEN?
3. Is there a way to power the AMPS down while keeping the PCM (and calibration) alive — e.g. a kcontrol, the amps' software-shutdown via the driver, runtime PM of the I2C device (and does runtime resume reload calibration)?
4. Propose the concrete logic: amps/PCM off when idle (at least on battery), on and calibrated when playback starts, event-driven (no polling). Compare (a) a WirePlumber 0.5 Lua hook on node state change, (b) an external watcher on `pactl subscribe` / `pw-mon`, (c) changing suspend-timeout per power source, (d) anything better. Give a minimal implementation sketch for the best option, its failure modes (pop/click, first 100 ms thin, race with first audio, ALSA state saved across reboot), and a verification plan that does not rely only on ears (e.g. enabling dynamic debug for the tas2781 modules to see calibration being written).
